  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Benign weather continues through the remainder of October with \nnear to above normal temperatures expected. \n\nSurface high pressure will continue to build south and west toward \nthe central Appalachians and Mid-South tonight. Meanwhile, low \npressure over the Canadian Maritimes will drive a weakened cold \nfront into the region from the north and west Tuesday. The front \nwill cross the area Tuesday morning before stalling/washing out to \nthe south/east Tuesday afternoon. By Wednesday morning, the same \nfront will retreat back to the north as a warm front with another \nlarge area of high pressure building to the north Thursday through \nSaturday. As a result, expect passing high clouds and a series of \nwind shifts with each of the frontal passages. Winds will remain \nlight out of the north and northwest this evening through Tuesday. \nWinds will change to the south and southwest late Wednesday through \nFriday. This will yield seasonable to above normal temperatures for \nthe remainder of the workweek. Highs will generally range from the \nmid 60s and low 70s though Wednesday with low to mid 70s Thursday \nand mid to upper 70s Friday. This is thanks to the position of the \nhigh to the north leading to increased southeast to southwest winds \nacross the region. Overnight lows will remain warm as well in the \nmid 40s and low 50s. \n\nDry conditions will continue through Saturday although this will be \na day of transition as the pattern begins to loosen. Upper level \nridging will buckle with an incoming trough from the Midwest \nregion. This trough will ultimately bring an end to the dry \nconditions with increasing cloud cover and precipitation chances \nheading into the first few days of November. Despite the increased \ncloud cover, expect highs Saturday back in the upper 60s and low \n70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\nUpper-level ridging overhead breaks down into zonal flow beginning \nSaturday into Sunday. Conditions likely start to become \ncloudier throughout the day Saturday as troughing develops over \nthe Ohio River Valley before heading east Sunday into Monday. \nThis likely brings the first chances for precipitation over the \nMid- Atlantic in over a week, although overall breadth and total\nrainfall from this system remain uncertain at this time. \n\nTemperatures likely drop back to the low-70s over the weekend \nin the wake of another weak front. With overnight lows \ngenerally remaining in the 40s and 50s, this ranges somewhat \nabove normal for the beginning of November. Looking ahead, model\nguidance hints at a second, potentially more potent upper-level\ntrough approaching the area that could bring additional chances\nfor precipitation sometime next week.",
